================
THE SHAPE RULE HOLDS AT THE SHIPPED WORKER COUNT, 9 DRAWS TO 0.

ARM A -- the untested band, WORKERS=1, M=8 against M=1:
    P23  pu 2.79   -7.5%  -6.8%
    P11  pu 2.14   -8.6%  -9.5%
Four for four.  The boundary is now bracketed between 1.23 (loses 5.7%) and 2.14 (wins 8.6%).

ARM B -- nw=3, OGC_MSET pinned so every worker takes the same M:
            pu     MSET=1        MSET=8        delta     per draw
    P36   4.66    97,247,964    79,368,748    -18.4%    -18.4 -17.8 -18.4
    P13   4.09    81,782,204    72,734,312    -11.1%    -12.8 -10.2  -9.1
    P2    3.29    74,602,257    57,836,096    -22.5%    -21.2 -22.5 -22.5
    P16   1.23     2,956,462     3,470,993    +17.4%     +8.6 +18.8 +17.4
    P3    1.18     4,468,072     4,616,676    + 3.3%     +5.2  +3.3  +2.1
    P1    1.02       571,668       676,903    +18.4%     +4.6 +26.2 +30.5

    pu >= 2.0   -17.23%        pu < 2.0   +9.61%

NINE DRAWS ONE WAY AND NINE THE OTHER, WITH NO SIGN FLIP ANYWHERE.  Against the 17-35% run-to-run
spread every wall-clock experiment fought today, this is a different order of evidence.

WHAT THE RULE IS.  peak_util >= 2.0 -> OGC_MCAND=8 on every worker; below it, touch nothing.
Leaving the low side alone matters: P1, P3 and P16 then run byte-identical to what is submitted, so
the change has no path to cost anything on the instances it does not fire on.

WHY IT WORKS, in the file's own words.  ogc_fast.cpp at the level loop: "The permutation is the
largest lever measured on this problem -- cdecomp put the construction spread across orders at
32-210%, against about 2% for the combined range of budget policy, axis sets, the w3mul grid and
brk removal."  OGC_MCAND is what opens that dimension -- each state expands the M earliest UNPLACED
blocks rather than only order[level], so states differ in WHICH blocks they hold, not just where.
The dedup right below it calls itself inert precisely because the shipped M=1 makes every state at
level k hold the same block set.  With M>1 it starts doing work.

Where the yard is saturated, which block goes next decides the layout and depth cannot recover it.
Where it is not, depth is everything and branching only costs draws.  That is the whole shape
dependence, and peak_util is exactly a saturation measure.

STILL OUTSTANDING BEFORE SHIPPING.  The control above is MSET=1, and the SHIPPED default is
MSET="1,2" indexed by wid%2 -- one worker in three already runs M=2, so part of this is already
collected.  The real shipping A/B is default against rule, and that is what runs next.
